---
// About me, with experience as the output of git log --graph (src/scripts/home/gitlog.js colours it and expands commits).
import Buffer from "../frame/Buffer.astro";
import "../../styles/home/about.css";
---
<Buffer id="about" file="whoami.pdf" mode="PDFView" meta="revised June 2026">
    <h2 class="h2">About me</h2>
    <p class="prose">I study computer science at Stellenbosch University, and I’ve been a teaching assistant for introductory computer science and for computer architecture. I like knowing what’s going on underneath the code I write, right down to the logic gates, and I like the maths behind it too. I’m happiest when I get to build something myself, and so far that’s been a scheduler, a compiler and an 8-bit computer on breadboards. Outside of all that, I’m a linguistics nerd and I’m into cars.</p>

    {/* Experience, as the one-line git log that would print it: "<date> - (refs) <subject> <author>".
         data-lanes colours each graph character by branch: m main, u undergrad, t teaching-assistant,
         b bbd-software, i mwr-internship (the colours are set in src/styles/home/about.css). data-cont is the graph that continues below a commit, and a
         .gl-desc becomes its expandable description. data-branch colours the date; data-tip makes the
         * bold for a branch that's still going; data-today swaps in the current month when the page loads.
         Without JavaScript this shows as plain text. */}
    <h3 class="ds-h gitlog-cmd"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>git log --graph --all --color \
    --date=format:'%Y-%m' \
    --format='%ad -%C(auto)%d%C(reset) %s %C(blue)&lt;%an&gt;%C(reset)'</h3>
